Integrated public-source qualification
=====================================

This snapshot combines the locally reviewed gateway, device, credential and
backup changes prepared after the last completed qualification candidate,
together with the HLS election startup repair and explicit unsupported backup
outcomes. The frozen source manifests, individual regression evidence and
original failed qualification runs remain retained. Combining the source does
not convert any incomplete earlier VM run into a pass.

An earlier full backend run stopped with 10,864 passes, 230 skips and 18 setup
errors in a concurrent HLS worker fixture. Its dependent queues stopped before
starting later candidates. The integrated snapshot must independently pass the
complete shipping backend suite, frontend tests/lint/strict build, installation
and recovery checks, browser routes, image scans and native-binary controls.
Each result must bind to the integrated source or its exact built image.

Local regression fixtures and synthetic peers are distinguished from real API
requests, packaged runtime tests and owner-reported hardware use. Historical
per-candidate independent shipping probes that did not run remain incomplete;
the integrated full suite is new evidence, not replacement receipts for those
probes. Dependency scanning is a dated check of known advisories, not proof of
the absence of all vulnerabilities. Existing typing and static-analysis debt
remain recorded rather than hidden by the release process.

No document or passing local gate here constitutes an enterprise-production
certification. Hosted CI must actually execute; signed public image publication
and independent installation need verification; controlled hardware writes,
customer restore exercises, sustained soak testing and measured recovery
objectives require separate evidence. The supported qualification scope remains
Linux amd64 on one host. ARM, cross-host HA, zero-RPO and enterprise SLAs are not
claimed. The supported operator and maintenance scope must be stated plainly
before a public release.

Gateway export and cache follow-up (2026-09-25)
--------------------------------------------
The later integrated candidate combines the separately reviewed OpenWrt CGI
export transport, its dedicated browser control and gateway inventory-cache
authorization. Their original local results remain attached to their exact
source snapshots. Three waiting supervisors were deliberately retired before
any of them launched VM work. They are not presented as individually completed
shipping qualifications. The active predecessor's VM run was left unchanged.

The combined regression baseline has 108 added backend cases: 90 failures on
the predecessor source and 18 controls. The latest local after-state passes
1,188 related PostgreSQL checks and includes every combined backend source byte.
The 72 independent cache HTTP checks, 40 OpenWrt TLS checks and 13 dedicated-page
Chromium checks retain their separate origins and synthetic-peer limitations.
All frontend bytes are unchanged from the dedicated-page correction. Fresh full
shipping backend, Alpine frontend, runtime/browser, core-image and native-binary
checks are still required for this integrated candidate before its branch push.

See OPENWRT-CONFIG-TRANSPORT, OPENWRT-EXPORT-UI and GATEWAY-CACHE-AUTHORITY for the
changes and their limits. This combination does not add managed gateway backup
storage, retention or restore, and does not close the external hardware, hosted
CI, signed-publication or operational acceptance gates described above.
